  
1895 Problem:-

The integers in the magic square do not have to be distinct. Every 1 x 1 grid is trivially a magic square.
Given an m x n integer grid, return the size (i.e., the side length k) of the largest magic square that can be found within this grid.

Example 1:
Input: grid = [[7,1,4,5,6],[2,5,1,6,4],[1,5,4,3,2],[1,2,7,3,4]]
Output: 3
Explanation: The largest magic square has a size of 3.
Every row sum, column sum, and diagonal sum of this magic square is equal to 12.
- Row sums: 5+1+6 = 5+4+3 = 2+7+3 = 12
- Column sums: 5+5+2 = 1+4+7 = 6+3+3 = 12
- Diagonal sums: 5+4+3 = 6+4+2 = 12

Example 2:
Input: grid = [[5,1,3,1],[9,3,3,1],[1,3,3,8]]
Output: 2
 
Constraints:-
m == grid.length
n == grid[i].length
1 <= m, n <= 50
1 <= grid[i][j] <= 106

===================================================================================================================================

Solution:-

Intuition:-
A magic square requires all rows, all columns, and both diagonals to have the same sum.
Checking these sums repeatedly for every possible sub-square would be inefficient.
To optimize this, we precompute prefix sums for rows, columns, and both diagonals.
This allows us to calculate any required sum in O(1) time, making validation of each square fast.

Approach:-
Precompute Prefix Sums
Build four 2D prefix sum arrays:
rowSum for row range sums
colSum for column range sums
diag for main diagonal sums
antidiag for anti-diagonal sums
Try Square Sizes from Large to Small
Iterate square size k from min(r, c) down to 2
The first valid square found is the largest one
Validate Each k x k Sub-square
Compare main diagonal and anti-diagonal sums
If they match, check:
All k rows have the same sum
All k columns have the same sum
Early Exit
Return k immediately when a valid magic square is found
Fallback Case
If no square of size ≥ 2 exists, return 1 (a single cell is always magic)

Complexity:-
Time Complexity: O(min(r, c)^3)
Trying all square sizes k
For each size, scanning all possible positions
Checking k rows and columns per position
Space Complexity: O(r * c)
Four prefix sum matrices

Code:-
class Solution {
    public int largestMagicSquare(int[][] grid) {
        int r = grid.length, c = grid[0].length;
        int[][] rowSum = new int[r + 1][c + 1];
        int[][] colSum = new int[r + 1][c + 1];
        int[][] diag = new int[r + 1][c + 1];
        int[][] antidiag = new int[r + 1][c + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < r; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < c; j++) {
                int x = grid[i][j];
                rowSum[i + 1][j + 1] = rowSum[i + 1][j] + x;
                colSum[i + 1][j + 1] = colSum[i][j + 1] + x;
                diag[i + 1][j + 1] = diag[i][j] + x;
                antidiag[i + 1][j] = antidiag[i][j + 1] + x;
            }
        }

        for (int k = Math.min(r, c); k >= 2; k--) {
            if (isMagic(k, r, c, rowSum, colSum, diag, antidiag)) 
                return k;
        }
        return 1;
    }

    private boolean isMagic(int k, int r, int c, int[][] rowSum, int[][] colSum, int[][] diag, int[][] antidiag) {
        for (int i = 0; i <= r - k; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= c - k; j++) {
                int s = diag[i + k][j + k] - diag[i][j];
                int anti = antidiag[i + k][j] - antidiag[i][j + k];
                if (s != anti) 
                    continue;
                boolean valid = true;
                for (int m = 0; m < k; m++) {
                    int row = rowSum[i + m + 1][j + k] - rowSum[i + m + 1][j];
                    int col = colSum[i + k][j + m + 1] - colSum[i][j + m + 1];
                    if (row != s || col != s) {
                        valid = false; break; 
                    }
                }
                if (valid) 
                    return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
